Those numbers are already a pair of geographic coordinates – latitude 37.4224764 and longitude ‑122.0842499.  
If you feed that pair into a reverse‑geocoding request (the “Geocoding API” that turns coordinates into a human‑readable address), you’ll get back an address that points to the **Googleplex** in Mountain View, California.

Below is a quick example of how you would make that request with the Google Maps Geocoding API (you’ll need a valid API key):

```http
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

A successful response will look something like this:

```json
{
   "results" : [
      {
         "address_components" : [
            { "long_name" : "1600", "short_name" : "1600", "types" : ["street_number"] },
            { "long_name" : "Amphitheatre Parkway", "short_name" : "Amphitheatre Pkwy", "types" : ["route"] },
            { "long_name" : "Mountain View", "short_name" : "Mountain View", "types" : ["locality","political"] },
            { "long_name" : "Santa Clara County", "short_name" : "Santa Clara County", "types" : ["administrative_area_level_2","political"] },
            { "long_name" : "California", "short_name" : "CA", "types" : ["administrative_area_level_1","political"] },
            { "long_name" : "United States", "short_name" : "US", "types" : ["country","political"] },
            { "long_name" : "94043", "short_name" : "94043", "types" : ["postal_code"] }
         ],
         "formatted_address" : "1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA",
         "geometry" : {
            "location" : { "lat" : 37.4224764, "lng" : -122.0842499 },
            "location_type" : "ROOFTOP",
            "viewport" : {
               "northeast" : { "lat" : 37.4238253802915, "lng" : -122.0829009197085 },
               "southwest" : { "lat" : 37.4211274197085, "lng" : -122.0855988802915 }
            }
         },
         "place_id" : "ChIJj61dQgK6j4AR4Z7x8YfXc3g",
         "types" : [ "street_address" ]
      }
   ],
   "status" : "OK"
}
```

So, to answer your question directly: the coordinates you supplied are indeed the latitude and longitude for the Googleplex. If you want to verify or retrieve additional details (like the formatted address, place ID, etc.), you’d use the reverse‑geocoding endpoint shown above.
